@node	3.5.1, 3.5.2, 3.5, 3.5
@subsection Streams Are Delayed Lists

As we saw in @ref{2.2.3}, sequences can serve as standard interfaces for
combining program modules.  We formulated powerful abstractions for
manipulating sequences, such as @code{map}, @code{filter}, and @code{fold},
that capture a wide variety of operations in a manner that is both succinct
and elegant.  Unfortunately, if we represent sequences as lists, this elegance
is bought at the price of severe inflexibility with respect to the time and
space required by our computations.

When we represent manipulations on sequences as transformations of the lists,
our programs must construct and copy data structures (which may be huge) at
every step of a process.  To see why this is true, let us compare two
programs for computing the sum of all the prime numbers in an interval.  The
first program is written in standard iterative style:@footnote{We assume here
that we have a predicate @code{is_prime} for testing primality, such as the
one we saw in @ref{1.2.6}.}

@example rust
fn sum_primes_list(a: i128, b: i128) -> i128 @{
    let mut primes_found = Vec::new();
    for n in a..=b @{
        if is_prime(n, &primes()) @{
            primes_found.push(n);
        @}
    @}
    primes_found.iter().sum()
@}
@end example

@noindent
The second program performs the same computation using the sequence operations
of @ref{2.2.3}:

@example rust
fn sum_primes(a: i128, b: i128) -> i128 @{
    stream_filter(|n| is_prime(*n, &primes()), &stream_enumerate_interval(a, b))
        .iter()
        .sum()
@}
@end example

@noindent
In carrying out the computation, the first program needs to store only the sum
being accumulated.  In contrast, the filter in the second program cannot do
any testing until @code{stream_enumerate_interval} has constructed a complete
list of the numbers in the interval.  The filter generates another list, which
in turn is passed to the folding step before being collapsed to form a sum.
Such large intermediate storage is not needed by the first program, which we
can think of as enumerating the interval incrementally, adding each prime to
the sum as it is generated.  The inefficiency in using lists becomes painfully
apparent if we use the sequence paradigm to compute the second prime in the
interval from 10,000 to 1,000,000 by evaluating the expression

@example rust
let primes_found: Vec<i128> = (10_000..=1_000_000)
    .filter(|n| is_prime(*n, &primes()))
    .collect();

let second_prime = primes_found[1];
@end example

@noindent
This expression does find the second prime, but the computational overhead is
outrageous.  We construct a list of almost a million integers, filter this
list by testing each element for primality, and then ignore almost all of the
result.  In a more traditional programming style, we would interleave the
enumeration and the filtering, and stop when we've reached the second prime.

Streams are a clever idea that allows one to use sequence manipulations
without incurring the costs of manipulating sequences as lists.  With streams
we can achieve the best of both worlds: we can formulate programs elegantly as
sequence manipulations, while attaining the efficiency of incremental
computation.  The basic idea is to arrange to construct a stream only
partially, and to pass the partial construction to the program that consumes
the stream.  If the consumer attempts to access a part of the stream that has
not yet been constructed, the stream will automatically construct just enough
more of itself to produce the required part, thus preserving the illusion that
the entire stream exists.  In other words, although we will write our programs
as if we were processing complete sequences, we design our stream
implementation to automatically and transparently interleave the construction
of the stream with its use.

On the surface, streams are just lists with different names for the procedures
that manipulate them.  There is a constructor, @code{cons_stream}, and two
selectors, @code{stream_head} and @code{stream_tail}, which satisfy the
constraints

@example rust
stream_head(&cons_stream(x, tail_thunk)) == &x
stream_tail(&cons_stream(x, tail_thunk)) == tail_thunk()
@end example

@noindent
There is a distinguishable object, @code{Stream::Empty}, the empty stream,
which cannot be the result of any @code{cons_stream} operation, and which can
be identified with the predicate @code{is_empty}.@footnote{In this edition
the empty stream is a constructor of the stream type, so
@code{stream_null?} in the book's Scheme is the method
@code{is_empty}: @code{s.is_empty()}.}  Thus we can make and use streams, in
just the same way as we can make and use lists, to represent aggregate data
arranged in a sequence.  In particular, we can build stream analogs of the
list operations from @ref{Chapter 2}, such as @code{stream_ref}, @code{stream_map}, and
@code{stream_for_each}:@footnote{This should bother you.  The fact that we are
defining such similar procedures for streams and lists indicates that we are
missing some underlying abstraction.  Unfortunately, to exploit this abstraction, we will need to exert finer control over the process of
evaluation than we can at present, which we will discuss further at the end of
@ref{3.5.4}.  In @ref{4.2}, we'll develop a framework that unifies lists and
streams.}

@example rust
fn stream_ref<A: Clone + 'static>(s: &Stream<A>, n: usize) -> A @{
    if n == 0 @{
        return s.head().clone();
    @}
    stream_ref(&s.tail(), n - 1)
@}

fn stream_map<A, B, F>(f: F, s: &Stream<A>) -> Stream<B>
where
    A: Clone + 'static,
    B: 'static,
    F: Fn(&A) -> B + Clone + 'static,
@{
    if s.is_empty() @{
        return Stream::Empty;
    @}
    let head = f(s.head());
    let source = s.clone();
    cons_stream(head, move || stream_map(f, &source.tail()))
@}

fn stream_for_each<A, F>(mut proc: F, s: &Stream<A>)
where
    A: Clone + 'static,
    F: FnMut(&A),
@{
    if s.is_empty() @{
        return;
    @}
    proc(s.head());
    stream_for_each(proc, &s.tail());
@}
@end example

@noindent
@code{Stream_for_each} is useful for viewing streams:

@example rust
fn display_line<A: Display>(x: &A) -> String @{
    format!(@{x@})
@}

fn display_stream<A>(s: &Stream<A>) -> Vec<String>
where
    A: Display + Clone + 'static,
@{
    let mut lines = Vec::new();
    stream_for_each(|x| lines.push(format!(@{x@})), s);
    lines
@}
@end example

@noindent
Rust ships its own lazy sequences: the @code{Iterator} trait, and the
@code{iter()} cursor this chapter uses to drain a bounded prefix of a stream
into a test.  An iterator is a one-shot cursor: two cursors over it cannot
share one memoized spine, it cannot name its own earlier elements, and it
cannot be produced before its input exists, which is exactly what the
feedback loops of @ref{3.5.4} demand.  The book's stream makes the sharing and
the demand-driven construction explicit, and the exercises of this section
measure the difference; the cold @code{Iterator} is a comparison here and
carries no book example of its own.

@noindent
To make the stream implementation automatically and transparently interleave
the construction of a stream with its use, we will arrange for the
@code{tail} of a stream to be evaluated when it is accessed by the
@code{stream_tail} procedure rather than when the stream is constructed by
@code{cons_stream}.  This implementation choice is reminiscent of our
discussion of rational numbers in @ref{2.1.2}, where we saw that we can choose
to implement rational numbers so that the reduction of numerator and
denominator to lowest terms is performed either at construction time or at
selection time.  The two rational-number implementations produce the same data
abstraction, but the choice has an effect on efficiency.  There is a similar
relationship between streams and ordinary lists.  As data abstractions,
streams are the same as lists.  The difference is the time at which the
elements are evaluated.  With ordinary lists, both the @code{cons} and the
elements are evaluated at construction time.  With streams, the
@code{cons_stream} is evaluated at construction time.  Our implementation of
streams will be based on a special form called @code{delay}.  Evaluating
@code{(delay ⟨@var{exp}⟩)} does not evaluate the expression
@code{⟨@var{exp}⟩}, but rather returns a so-called
@newterm{delayed object}, which we can think of as a ``promise'' to evaluate
@code{⟨@var{exp}⟩} at some future time.  As a companion to
@code{delay}, there is a procedure called @code{force} that takes a delayed
object as an argument and performs the evaluation---in effect, forcing the
@code{delay} to fulfill its promise.  We will see below how @code{delay} and
@code{force} can be implemented, but first let us use these to construct
streams.

@code{Cons_stream} is defined so that @code{(cons-stream ⟨@var{a}⟩ ⟨@var{b}⟩)}
is equivalent to @code{(cons ⟨@var{a}⟩ (delay ⟨@var{b}⟩))}.
What this means is that we will construct streams using
the constructor @code{cons_stream}, whose second argument is a thunk: in Rust
the delay is explicit, because a plain argument would be evaluated before the
call, which is the one thing @code{cons_stream} exists to prevent.

@example rust
pub fn cons_stream<A: 'static>(
    head: A,
    tail: impl FnOnce() -> Stream<A> + 'static,
) -> Stream<A> @{
    Stream::cons_stream(head, tail)
@}
@end example

@noindent
@code{Stream_head} and @code{stream_tail} can now be defined as procedures:

@example rust
pub fn stream_head<A: 'static>(s: &Stream<A>) -> &A @{
    s.head()
@}

pub fn stream_tail<A: Clone + 'static>(s: &Stream<A>) -> Stream<A> @{
    s.tail()
@}
@end example

@noindent
@code{Stream_tail} selects the tail of the pair and forces the delayed
expression found there to obtain the rest of the stream.@footnote{Although
@code{stream_head} and @code{stream_tail} can be defined as ordinary
functions, @code{cons_stream} must take the tail as a thunk.  If
@code{cons_stream} took an eagerly evaluated tail, then, by the model of
evaluation, evaluating @code{(cons-stream ⟨@var{a}⟩ ⟨@var{b}⟩)}
would automatically cause @code{⟨@var{b}⟩} to be evaluated, which
is precisely what we do not want to happen.  For the same reason, the
@code{delay} of this edition is a function taking a closure, though
@code{force} is an ordinary procedure.}  The stream implementation in action
is below.  To see how this implementation behaves, let us analyze the
``outrageous'' computation we saw above, reformulated in terms of streams:

@example rust
stream_head(&stream_tail(&stream_filter(
    prime,
    &stream_enumerate_interval(10_000, 1_000_000),
)))
@end example

@noindent
We will see that it does indeed work efficiently.

We begin by calling @code{stream_enumerate_interval} with the arguments 10,000
and 1,000,000.  @code{Stream_enumerate_interval} is the stream analog of
@code{enumerate_interval} (@ref{2.2.3}):

@example rust
fn stream_enumerate_interval(low: i128, high: i128) -> Stream<i128> @{
    if low > high @{
        return Stream::Empty;
    @}
    cons_stream(low, move || stream_enumerate_interval(low + 1, high))
@}
@end example

@noindent
and thus the result returned by @code{stream_enumerate_interval}, formed by
@code{cons_stream}, is@footnote{The numbers shown here do not really appear
in the delayed expression.  What actually appears is the original expression,
in an environment in which the variables are bound to the appropriate
numbers.  For example, @code{(+ low 1)} with @code{low} bound to 10,000
actually appears where @code{10001} is shown.}

@example rust
cons_stream(10_000, || stream_enumerate_interval(10_001, 1_000_000))
@end example

@noindent
That is, @code{stream_enumerate_interval} returns a stream, represented as a
pair whose @code{head} is 10,000 and whose @code{tail} is a promise to
enumerate more of the interval if so requested.  This stream is now filtered
for primes using the stream analog of the @code{filter} procedure
(@ref{2.2.3}):

@example rust
fn stream_filter<A, F>(pred: F, s: &Stream<A>) -> Stream<A>
where
    A: Clone + 'static,
    F: Fn(&A) -> bool + Clone + 'static,
@{
    if s.is_empty() @{
        return Stream::Empty;
    @}
    let head = s.head().clone();
    if pred(&head) @{
        let source = s.clone();
        return cons_stream(head, move || stream_filter(pred, &source.tail()));
    @}
    stream_filter(pred, &s.tail())
@}
@end example

@noindent
@code{Stream_filter} tests the @code{head} of the stream (the @code{head}
of the pair, which is 10,000).  Since this is not prime,
@code{stream_filter} examines the @code{tail} of its input stream.  The call
to @code{stream_tail} forces evaluation of the delayed
@code{stream_enumerate_interval}, which returns another stream (beginning
with 10,001).  @code{Stream_filter} now looks at the @code{head} of this
stream, sees that this is not prime either, forces another
@code{stream_tail}, and so on, until @code{stream_enumerate_interval} yields
the prime 10,007, whereupon @code{stream_filter}, according to its
@code{cons_stream}, returns

@example rust
cons_stream(
    10_007,
    || stream_filter(prime, &stream_enumerate_interval(10_008, 1_000_000)),
)
@end example

@noindent
This result is now passed to @code{stream_tail} in our original expression.
This forces the delayed @code{stream_filter}, which in turn keeps forcing the
delayed @code{stream_enumerate_interval} until it finds the next prime,
which is 10,009.  Finally, the result passed to @code{stream_head} in our
original expression is

@example rust
cons_stream(
    10_009,
    || stream_filter(prime, &stream_enumerate_interval(10_010, 1_000_000)),
)
@end example

@noindent
@code{Stream_head} returns 10,009, and the computation is complete.  Only as
many integers were tested for primality as were necessary to find the second
prime, and the interval was enumerated only as far as was necessary to feed
the prime filter.  In general, we can think of delayed evaluation as
``demand-driven'' programming, whereby each stage in the stream process is
activated only enough to satisfy the next stage.  What we have done is to
decouple the actual order of events in the computation from the apparent
structure of our procedures.  We write procedures as if the streams existed
``all at once'' when, in reality, the computation is performed
incrementally, as in traditional programming styles.

@subsubheading Implementing @code{delay} and @code{force}

Although @code{delay} and @code{force} may seem like mysterious operations,
their implementation is really quite straightforward.  @code{Delay} must
package an expression so that it can be evaluated later on demand, and we can
accomplish this simply by treating the expression as the body of a
procedure.  @code{Delay} cannot be a special form such that
@code{(delay ⟨@var{exp}⟩)} is syntactic sugar for

@example
(lambda () ⟨@var{exp}⟩)
@end example

@noindent
because in Rust a function call evaluates its arguments first; the delay of
this edition is a function taking a closure, and forcing it is a call:

@example rust
pub fn delay<T: 'static, F: FnOnce() -> T + 'static>(f: F) -> Lazy<T> @{
    Lazy::new(f)
@}

#[must_use]
pub fn force<T: 'static>(promise: &Lazy<T>) -> Rc<T> @{
    promise.force()
@}
@end example

@noindent
This implementation suffices for @code{delay} and @code{force} to work as
advertised, but there is an important optimization that we can include.  In
many applications, we end up forcing the same delayed object many times.
This can lead to serious inefficiency in recursive programs involving
streams.  (See @ref{Exercise 3.57}.)  The solution is to build delayed objects
so that the first time they are forced, they store the value that is
computed.  Subsequent forcings will simply return the stored value without
repeating the computation.  In other words, we implement @code{delay} as a
special-purpose memoized procedure similar to the one described in
@ref{Exercise 3.27}.  One way to accomplish this is to use the following
procedure, which takes as argument a procedure (of no arguments) and returns
a memoized version of the procedure.  The first time the memoized procedure
is run, it saves the computed result.  On subsequent evaluations, it simply
returns the result.

@example rust
fn memo_proc<T>(proc: impl FnOnce() -> T + Clone + 'static) -> impl Fn() -> T @{
    let already_run = Rc::new(Cell::new(false));
    let result: Rc<RefCell<Option<T>>> = Rc::new(RefCell::new(None));
    move || @{
        if !already_run.get() @{
            *result.borrow_mut() = Some(proc());
            already_run.set(true);
        @}
        result.borrow().clone().expect("memoized by the arm above")
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
The runtime's @code{Lazy} of this edition realizes exactly this memoized
procedure, with the generator dropped after the first run so a self-referential
stream cannot force it twice:

@example rust
pub struct Lazy<T> @{
    cell: Rc<LazyCell<T>>,
@}

struct LazyCell<T> @{
    f: RefCell<Option<Box<dyn FnOnce() -> T>>>,
    memo: RefCell<Option<Rc<T>>>,
@}

impl<T: 'static> Lazy<T> @{
    pub fn force(&self) -> Rc<T> @{
        if let Some(v) = &*self.cell.memo.borrow() @{
            return Rc::clone(v);
        @}
        let f = self.cell.f.borrow_mut().take();
        let value = Rc::new(f.expect("forced reentrantly")());
        *self.cell.memo.borrow_mut() = Some(Rc::clone(&value));
        value
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
Thus @code{(delay ⟨@var{exp}⟩)} of the book is
@code{delay(|| ⟨@var{exp}⟩)} over this @code{Lazy}, and
@code{force} is as defined previously.@footnote{There are many possible
implementations of streams other than the one described in this section.
Delayed evaluation, which is the key to making streams practical, was inherent
in Algol 60's @newterm{call-by-name} parameter-passing method.  The use of this
mechanism to implement streams was first described by @ref{Landin (1965)}.  Delayed
evaluation for streams was introduced into Lisp by @ref{Friedman and Wise (1976)}. In
their implementation, @code{cons} always delays evaluating its arguments, so
that lists automatically behave as streams.  The memoizing optimization is also
known as @newterm{call-by-need}.  The Algol community would refer to our
original delayed objects as @newterm{call-by-name thunks} and to the optimized
versions as @newterm{call-by-need thunks}.}

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.50}Exercise 3.50:} Complete the following
definition, which generalizes @code{stream_map} to allow procedures that take
multiple arguments, analogous to @code{map} in @ref{2.2.1}, 
@ref{Footnote 78}.  In this edition the list of argument streams becomes a
slice of streams.

@example rust
fn stream_map_n<A, B, F>(f: F, streams: &[Stream<A>]) -> Stream<B>
where
    A: Clone + 'static,
    B: 'static,
    F: Fn(&[A]) -> B + Clone + 'static,
@{
    if streams.iter().@r{⟨??⟩}(|s| s.is_empty()) @{
        return Stream::Empty;
    @}
    let heads: Vec<A> = streams.iter().map(|s| s.head().clone()).collect();
    let tails: Vec<Stream<A>> = streams.iter().map(|s| s.tail()).collect();
    cons_stream(f(&heads), move || stream_map_n(f, @r{⟨??⟩}))
@}
@end example

@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.51}Exercise 3.51:} To take a closer look at
delayed evaluation, we will use the following procedure, which simply returns
its argument after printing it (here @code{show} records the value in a log
the demonstration can inspect, so the evidence is observable in library code
that does not print):

@example rust
fn show<A: Display>(x: A, log: &Rc<RefCell<Vec<String>>>) -> A @{
    log.borrow_mut().push(format!("@{x@}"));
    x
@}
@end example

What does the interpreter print in response to evaluating each expression in
the following sequence?@footnote{Exercises such as @ref{Exercise 3.51} and
@ref{Exercise 3.52} are valuable for testing our understanding of how
@code{delay} works.  On the other hand, intermixing delayed evaluation with
printing---and, even worse, with assignment---is extremely confusing, and
instructors of courses on computer languages have traditionally tormented their
students with examination questions such as the ones in this section.  Needless
to say, writing programs that depend on such subtleties is odious programming
style.  Part of the power of stream processing is that it lets us ignore the
order in which events actually happen in our programs.  Unfortunately, this is
precisely what we cannot afford to do in the presence of assignment, which
forces us to be concerned with time and change.}

@example rust
let log = Rc::new(RefCell::new(Vec::new()));
let x = stream_map(
    |v| show(*v, &log),
    &stream_map(|v| v * v, &stream_enumerate_interval(0, 10)),
);

stream_ref(&x, 5)
// => 25

stream_ref(&x, 7)
// => 49
@end example

@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.52}Exercise 3.52:} Consider the sequence of
expressions

@example rust
let sum = Rc::new(Cell::new(0i128));

let accum = @{
    let sum = Rc::clone(&sum);
    move |v: i128| @{
        sum.set(sum.get() + v);
        sum.get()
    @}
@};

let seq = stream_map(|v| accum(*v), &stream_enumerate_interval(1, 20));

let y = stream_filter(|v| v % 2 == 0, &seq);

let z = stream_filter(|v| v % 5 == 0, &seq);

stream_ref(&y, 7)
// => 136

display_stream(&z)
// => ["10", "15", "45", "55", "105", "120"]

sum.get()
// => 210
@end example

What is the value of @code{sum} after each of the above expressions is
evaluated?  What is the printed response to evaluating the @code{stream_ref}
and @code{display_stream} expressions?  Would these responses differ if we had
implemented @code{(delay ⟨@var{exp}⟩)} simply as @code{(lambda () ⟨@var{exp}⟩)}
without using the optimization provided by @code{memo_proc}?  Explain.
@end quotation

@node	3.5.2, 3.5.3, 3.5.1, 3.5
@subsection Infinite Streams

We have seen how to support the illusion of manipulating streams as complete
entities even though, in actuality, we compute only as much of the stream as we
need to access.  We can exploit this technique to represent sequences
efficiently as streams, even if the sequences are very long.  What is more
striking, we can use streams to represent sequences that are infinitely long.
For instance, consider the following definition of the stream of positive
integers:

@example rust
fn integers_starting_from(n: i128) -> Stream<i128> @{
    cons_stream(n, move || integers_starting_from(n + 1))
@}

let integers = integers_starting_from(1);
@end example

@noindent
This makes sense because @code{integers} will be a pair whose @code{head} is 1
and whose @code{tail} is a promise to produce the integers beginning with 2.
This is an infinitely long stream, but in any given time we can examine only a
finite portion of it.  Thus, our programs will never know that the entire
infinite stream is not there.

Using @code{integers} we can define other infinite streams, such as the stream
of integers that are not divisible by 7:

@example rust
fn divisible(x: i128, y: i128) -> bool @{
    x % y == 0
@}

let no_sevens = stream_filter(|x| !divisible(*x, 7), &integers());
@end example

@noindent
Then we can find integers not divisible by 7 simply by accessing elements of
this stream:

@example rust
stream_ref(&no_sevens, 100)
// => 117
@end example

@noindent
In analogy with @code{integers}, we can define the infinite stream of Fibonacci
numbers:

@example rust
fn fibgen(a: i128, b: i128) -> Stream<i128> @{
    cons_stream(a, move || fibgen(b, a + b))
@}

let fibs = fibgen(0, 1);
@end example

@noindent
@code{Fibs} is a pair whose @code{head} is 0 and whose @code{tail} is a promise
to evaluate @code{(fibgen 1 1)}.  When we evaluate this delayed @code{(fibgen 1
1)}, it will produce a pair whose @code{head} is 1 and whose @code{tail} is a
promise to evaluate @code{(fibgen 1 2)}, and so on.

For a look at a more exciting infinite stream, we can generalize the
@code{no_sevens} example to construct the infinite stream of prime numbers,
using a method known as the @newterm{sieve of Eratosthenes}.@footnote{Eratosthenes, a third-century @abbr{B.C.}
Alexandrian Greek philosopher, is famous for giving the first accurate estimate
of the circumference of the Earth, which he computed by observing shadows cast
at noon on the day of the summer solstice.  Eratosthenes's sieve method,
although ancient, has formed the basis for special-purpose hardware ``sieves''
that, until recently, were the most powerful tools in existence for locating
large primes.  Since the 70s, however, these methods have been superseded by
outgrowths of the probabilistic techniques discussed in @ref{1.2.6}.}
We start with the integers beginning with 2, which is the first prime.  To get
the rest of the primes, we start by filtering the multiples of 2 from the rest
of the integers.  This leaves a stream beginning with 3, which is the next
prime.  Now we filter the multiples of 3 from the rest of this stream.  This
leaves a stream beginning with 5, which is the next prime, and so on.  In other
words, we construct the primes by a sieving process, described as follows: To
sieve a stream @code{S}, form a stream whose first element is the first element
of @code{S} and the rest of which is obtained by filtering all multiples of the
first element of @code{S} out of the rest of @code{S} and sieving the
result. This process is readily described in terms of stream operations:

@example rust
fn sieve(s: &Stream<i128>) -> Stream<i128> @{
    let first = *s.head();
    let source = s.clone();
    cons_stream(first, move || @{
        sieve(&stream_filter(move |x| x % first != 0, &source.tail()))
    @})
@}

let primes = sieve(&integers_starting_from(2));
@end example

@noindent
Now to find a particular prime we need only ask for it:

@example rust
stream_ref(&primes, 50)
// => 233
@end example

@noindent
It is interesting to contemplate the signal-processing system set up by
@code{sieve}, shown in the ``Henderson diagram'' in 
@ref{Figure 3.31}.@footnote{We have named these figures after Peter Henderson, who was the
first person to show us diagrams of this sort as a way of thinking about stream
processing.  Each solid line represents a stream of values being transmitted.
The dashed line from the @code{head} to the @code{cons} and the @code{filter}
indicates that this is a single value rather than a stream.}  The input stream
feeds into an ``un@code{cons}er'' that separates the first element of the
stream from the rest of the stream.  The first element is used to construct a
divisibility filter, through which the rest is passed, and the output of the
filter is fed to another sieve box.  Then the original first element is
@code{cons}ed onto the output of the internal sieve to form the output stream.
Thus, not only is the stream infinite, but the signal processor is also
infinite, because the sieve contains a sieve within it.

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.31}
@ifinfo
@strong{Figure 3.31:} The prime sieve viewed as a signal-processing system.

@example
  +---------------------------------------------------------------+
  | sieve                                                         |
  |                                                               |
  |        __/|                                        |\__       |
  |     __/car|........................................|   \__    |
  |   _/      |           :                            |      \_  |
----><_       |           V                            |  cons _>---->
  |    \__    |    +------------+    +------------+    |    __/   |
  |       \cdr|--->| filter:    |    | sieve      |--->| __/      |
  |          \|    |            |--->|            |    |/         |
  |                | not        |    |            |               |
  |                | divisible? |    |            |               |
  |                +------------+    +------------+               |
  +---------------------------------------------------------------+
@end example

@end ifinfo
